I would like a script that does not require command line arguments, and will process these major folders: 

- Assignments
- Lecturese
- Resources
- Worksheets 

gleaning information from the subfolder's README file. Using that gleaned information, the script would, again, add a row for each subfolders' assignment to the major folders README keeping my repo organized and giving a nice synopsis of the contents (with links) to the major folders' contents. 

I previously used the top two heading lines in each subfolders README: 


```
## Test 1 - Various Topics
#### Due: 02-12-2025 (Wed @ 2:00)
``

and get a title, description, and a due date. This is obviously limiting. So a yaml block could be much more informative, but a pain to create, along with the fact that Github renders yaml in an ugly fashion. 

If I were to use yaml, I would want the following two major requirements:

1. defaults for all possible values
2. if a yaml block is not present, after the first run of the script it would create and add the yaml block with all the defaults and gleaned info (from the folder names). 

Below is an example yaml block that I would want to either create myself or have auto generated:

```yaml 
is_due: (bool) (default = false)
id: (determined by parent folder name)
name: (same as id)
title: (entered by me) (default = `NO TITLE`)
description: (entered by me) (default = `NO DESCRIPTION`)
category: (determined from the category folder it resides in [Assignments, Lectures, Resources, Worksheets] which would be two levels up.) (no default as the category folder must exist)
date_due: (if is_due = false then due_date fields are all N/A)
    month: (2 digits) (default = this month)
    day: (2 digits) (default = today)
    year: (4 digits) (default = this year)
    hour: (24hr time) (default = current hour)
```





Can you look over what I've written, and pepper me with questions?

course_folder = a git repo folder that also has the "major" folders within it (Assignments, Worksheets, etc.)

Last summary: 

I want to type: `readmees` in a course_folder. If it's not a course folder (see previous definition) then stop, and print usage. Usage will say: you are not in a course folder, either add a path to a folder, or change into a course folder and re-run.

If the course folder test passes, then process readmees as previously stated generating the md table organizing each major folder with the following table data: 

| Is Due | Folder | Title | Description | Date Due | Days Left |
| :----- | :----- | :---- | :---------- | -------: | :-------: |


Is Due : is this an assignment to be turned in?
Folder: the folder that the assignment lives in.
Title: Assignment, Lecture, Resource, Worksheet title.
Description: same as title but its a description.
Date Due: if it "is due", then a date should be present.
Days Left: calculated days left from current date (when readmees was run) to the due date. Result could be a negative number if overdue.


1. The reason I wrote this description is because I myself have confused myself with the different version. I like the simplicity of my old multi-folder scanning script, but I also realized it had some issues as would any script that gets patched over time. So I was angling for a fresh script. Feel free to use anything you find in /dev, as long as we create an autonomus version of the readmees script.
2. I'm ok with a single course update. I wrote the multi course version without knowledge of my own use cases. I found that I only work on one course at a time. The only use I can see for the multicourse update is to ping the "last updated" dates so the "days left" stays closer to accurate. 
3. Yes please keep it python. If small tweaks are necessary, I can dive in. Bash is not my forte. As you can see in #2, there may be a reason to update all courses in order to keep the last updated date more current. 
4. Thats a good question. I have a naming convention: A=Assignment, H=homework, P=project or program, T=test/exam, L=lecture, W=worksheet. I number the folders to keep them in an order that coincides with data assigned,  so that each folder will have a unique first three characters. I use the appropriate category letter [A,H,P,T,L,W] followed by the next available 2 digit number. So the third worksheet would be something like: `W03-blah-blah-blah`. 
5. I can change each of those by hand or delete them since each yaml value should have a default value. In fact, if your script detects the old style, it can simply plug in the default yaml key:values above them, so the headings stay, but are ignored by `readmees`.
6. Shit. Good question. Yes, if a field goes away in any yaml block, it should be placed back, with said default value. I would love to say error checking should be at a minimum, but we both know that just causes programs to fail.
7. Oooh. YES!! I did not know that was possible.
8. I was trying to simplify the code making it more robust? I figured if every category generated a table with the same columns, it would be logically simpler. Honestly, I'm not sure if it is more confusing to students or comforting to see the same columns in each readme's table. I will leave it up to you. So choose what would be normal understandable conventions. Your superpower is knowing what is done more often, so go that way.
9. I'm not sure about "bolded", but I do like the strikethrough for past due assignments. Whether all columns in a row link to the same target, or the first column only is a link to said target, is up to you. It may look cleaner that way. As far as bold, italics, and strikethrough I will concede to contemporary styling expectations.
10. Folders with due dates should place the items still due in descending order. When an item is past due, it reverts to ascending order. If this seems confusing, I am not stuck on a specific ordering. Feel free to simplify logic as long as the next item still due is at the top along with its "days left". In folders (like resources) date order is irrelevant. Order those by folder name. I still tend to prepend two digits to folder names to inforce order. But, whether its two digits or strings, no crazy order alterations are needed. 
11. How days left is calculated, is up to you. I think I ended up printing a decimal zero for "due today" but I like your idea of "DUE TODAY!" when days ==0.

